Task 4.4 – Prevent Recurrence (1 Mark)
The @Override annotation checks whether a method correctly overrides a method from its parent class.

For example:
@Override
public void mouseclicked(MouseEvent e) {
    // Code
}


If the method name is misspelled, the compiler displays an error similar to:
error: method does not override or implement
a method from a supertype


Without @Override, the incorrect method may compile, but the mouse event will not call it.

Therefore, using @Override helps identify spelling mistakes during compilation.
