Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
Instead of storing points in an ArrayList, we can use an off-screen BufferedImage.
1. BufferedImage approach
- Create a BufferedImage with the same width and height as the drawing area.
- When the user clicks, draw a dot directly onto the image.
- In paint(), copy the image onto the screen using drawImage().
- The image preserves all previous drawings.
Example Java code:
BufferedImage image =
    new BufferedImage(400, 300,
                      BufferedImage.TYPE_INT_ARGB);

public void mouseClicked(MouseEvent e) {

    Graphics g = image.getGraphics();

    g.fillOval(e.getX() - 3,
               e.getY() - 3, 6, 6);

    g.dispose();

    count++;
    status.setText("Clicks: " + count);

    repaint();
}

public void paint(Graphics g) {
    super.paint(g);
    g.drawImage(image, 0, 0, this);
}


Required import:
import java.awt.image.BufferedImage;


This is an alternative implementation fragment. It must be integrated into the DrawPad constructor and class, and the image must be created before drawing.
2. Complexity comparison
Let:
- n = Number of mouse clicks.
- W = Image width in pixels.
- H = Image height in pixels.
Feature	ArrayList of Points	BufferedImage
Store one click	O(1) amortised	O(1) for fixed-size drawing
Repaint time	O(n)	O(W × H) worst-case pixel copy
Space	O(n)	O(W × H) pixels
Redrawing	Draw every stored point	Copy one image
Best use	Small drawings	Large or complex drawings

3. Explanation
ArrayList approach:
- Stores each click position as a Point.
- During every repaint, the program visits all n points.
- Repaint time is O(n).
- Memory usage is O(n).
BufferedImage approach:
- Stores the drawing as pixels.
- The drawing is updated when a click occurs.
- During repaint, the image is copied to the screen.
- A straightforward full-image copy takes O(W × H) pixel work.
- Memory usage is O(W × H), approximately four bytes per pixel for an ARGB image.
For example, a 400 × 300 ARGB image requires approximately:
\[
400 \times 300 \times 4 = 480000 \text{ bytes}
\]

Approximately 469 KB, excluding object and image-management overhead.
Final Result
The event-handling defect was identified and corrected by using the proper mouseClicked() method. The repaired program stores click positions, updates the label and redraws all dots using paint(). The @Override annotation prevents incorrect method names. The BufferedImage alternative provides another way to preserve drawings during repainting.
